# SentinelDocs

SentinelDocs is an LLM security lab demonstrating how indirect prompt injection
can corrupt a document-based AI assistant—and how a stronger trust boundary
changes the outcome.

In the live experiment, a tampered vendor document changed a real status of
`Pending manual review` into a false answer that the vendor had `passed and was
approved`. The identical question, document, and model returned the correct
pending status in hardened mode.

## Attack result

| Vulnerable mode | Hardened mode |
| --- | --- |
| Trusted workflow instructions embedded in retrieved documents. | Treated retrieved documents as untrusted data. |
| **False result:** Vendor passed and is approved. | **Correct result:** Vendor is pending manual review. |
| Attack succeeded. | Same attack failed. |

![Vulnerable mode returns a false vendor approval](docs/images/vulnerable-result.jpg)

![Hardened mode preserves the pending review status](docs/images/hardened-result.jpg)

The screenshots replay the saved live OpenAI answers through the local interface
without making additional API calls. The original responses and timestamps are
preserved in the linked JSON evidence below.

### Why this matters

An attacker could place instructions inside a vendor questionnaire, support
ticket, résumé, webpage, or other document consumed by an AI system. If the
application tells the model to trust directives in retrieved content, that
content may alter a business decision without changing the underlying record.

The vulnerable mode is intentionally naïve: it treats its knowledge base as
approved and follows workflow directives found inside documents. Hardened mode
explicitly separates trusted application instructions from untrusted document
text. This prevented the demonstrated attack, but prompt instructions alone are
not a universal defense; sensitive actions still require deterministic
authorization outside the model.

## Architecture

```text
Browser -> Flask API -> document retrieval -> model -> output guard -> response
                     -> authorization -> fictional read-only ticket tool
```

